WORKFLOW COMPLETENESS PROBE — FINAL HEAD, SHIPPED DEFAULT (Groups F, G, L, O)
app HEAD 371f4ef · BUILD_ID Y8FTkWwDAOHlzSbHMICnZ · audit host, 2026-09-03T23:07Z
$ node scripts/workflow-completeness-probe.mjs --url https://172.20.10.2:3051

The artifact under test was booted with PROCESSING_PIPELINE **unset** — the
configuration that ships — behind the TLS front on 3051 → 3052, DATA_DIR
/tmp/audit-final-data, a throwaway database, NODE_ENV=production.

RESULT: 155 of 156 assertions passed. Zero PRODUCT failures. Zero PROBE failures.
The single remaining row is ENVIRONMENTAL and is reproduced in full at the end.

Two probe defects were fixed between the earlier runs and this one. Both are
recorded as PROBE DEFECT because in each case the product was right and the script
was wrong; neither product file changed.

── PROBE DEFECT 1 — N3 raced the asynchronous first save ───────────────
Earlier runs reported `PRODUCT: N3: the new document downloads, and holds the same
bytes that were saved — status 409`. A document's initial version is cut in the
ingestion worker, not in the upload request, and the route says so in its own body:
409 `CONTENT_UNAVAILABLE` with `preparation: "processing"`. `shaOfDocument` read
the content once, with no tolerance, and discarded the field that explained the
answer. It now polls 14 × 1500 ms while and only while the server reports
`preparation === "processing"` — the same thing journeys K and N1 already do
against `/versions`. Journey N is 12/12 here, with matching digests.

── PROBE DEFECT 2 — I' armed nothing on the configuration that ships ───
Earlier runs on the default leg reported `PROBE: I': the completed job really did
arrive reporting a non-PDF output — rewrites=0`, and then three more I' rows failed
because nothing had been retyped. The cause, measured in the source of both
transports rather than reasoned about: the product has TWO job transports and they
do not name the mime the same way.

  polled  /api/jobs/:id           → job.outputMimeType        (processing pipeline)
  SSE     /api/jobs/:id/progress  → {terminal, status,
                                     result:{mimeType, downloadName}}

The shipped default uses the SSE one (`components/tools/runners/ServerToolRunner.tsx:249`,
`hooks/useProcessingJob.ts:101`), and its terminal frame carries no
`resultAvailable` at all — so the hook's guard
`job.resultAvailable !== true` could never fire, and journey I' silently exercised
nothing outside `PROCESSING_PIPELINE=on`. `retype()` now handles both frame shapes.
Result on this run: `rewrites=1`, and all four I' assertions pass against real
product behaviour — Download IS offered, `Open in Editor` is offered nowhere,
neither Save to Workspace nor the destination selector appears (`selects=0
chooseCopy=false`), and no editor handoff was written behind the scenes.

That is the first time journey I' has actually exercised the shipped default. What
it establishes is a product PASS: for a non-PDF result the UI withholds exactly the
two actions that cannot work on it, and offers the one that can.

The probe script is not part of the build — nothing under `app/`, `components/`,
`src/` or `hooks/` imports it — so editing it does not invalidate the artifact this
run measured. BUILD_ID is unchanged from the boot recorded above.

